                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Simplicial homotopy groups are the basic invariants of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complexes"} Kan complexes
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+homotopy+theory"} simplicial homotopy theory
          %p
            Given that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
            is a special
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"} behaves like
            a combinatorial model for a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %em simplicial homotopy groups
            of a Kan complex are accordingly the combinatorial analog of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spheres"} spheres
            modulo maps from topological disks, they are maps from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+of+a+simplex"} boundary of a simplex
            modulo those from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex"} simplex
            itself.
          %p
            Accordingly, the definition of the discussion of simplicial homotopy groups is essentially literally the same as that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
            of topological spaces. One technical difference is for instance that the definition of the group structure is slightly more non-immediate for simplicial homotopy groups than for topological homotopy groups (see below).
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Recall the classical
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} model structure on simplicial sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a fibrant simplicial set, i.e. a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
          #UnderlyingSetsOfSimplicialHomotopyGroups.num_defn
